---
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import GuideLayout from "../../components/GuideLayout.astro";
import { watershedCodeTheme } from "../../styles/code-theme";
import { excerpt, section } from "../../lib/excerpt";
import appSource from "../../../../examples/retro_tutorial_lustre/src/retro_tutorial_lustre.gleam?raw";
import schemaSource from "../../../../examples/retro_tutorial_lustre/src/doc_schema.gleam?raw";
import gleamToml from "../../../../examples/retro_tutorial_lustre/gleam.toml?raw";

const devConstants = section(
  appSource,
  "/// These dev constants match",
  "pub fn main(",
);
const start = excerpt(appSource, "pub fn main(");
const connectEffect = excerpt(appSource, "fn init(");
const readiness = section(
  appSource,
  "    GotDocument(doc) -> {",
  "    EnsuredNotes(Ok(notes)) ->",
);
const bootstrap = excerpt(appSource, "fn bootstrap_effect(");
const assemble = excerpt(appSource, "fn assemble(");

const beam = `import gleam/erlang/process.{type Subject}
import watershed/map_kernel
import watershed_beam

// Blocks until the op history has replayed locally, then hands back the
// document — natural inside an OTP process, impossible on the event loop.
let assert Ok(doc) =
  watershed_beam.connect(
    host: "127.0.0.1",
    port: 4000,
    tenant: "dev-tenant",
    document: "retro-tutorial",
    token: token,
    user_id: "ada",
  )

// Root-map changes arrive as a Subject you select on, not as a callback.
let events: Subject(map_kernel.MapEvent) =
  watershed_beam.subscribe(watershed_beam.root(doc))`;

const layout = `retro_tutorial_lustre/
├── gleam.toml
├── package.json
├── pnpm-workspace.yaml
├── build.mjs
├── index.html
├── src/
│   ├── doc_schema.gleam            ← this sheet
│   ├── note.gleam                  ← step 03
│   ├── board.gleam                 ← step 03
│   ├── board_ops.gleam             ← steps 03 and 04
│   └── retro_tutorial_lustre.gleam ← every sheet
└── test/
    ├── note_codec_test.gleam
    ├── board_test.gleam
    └── convergence_test.gleam      ← step 06`;
---

<GuideLayout
  slug="connect"
  description="Step two of the watershed build guide: connect the tutorial retro board to a live document, treat the handle and the handshake as two separate events, and seed its register and tally OR-maps with ensure_or_map before the first render."
>
  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>The races were real; the sequencer wasn’t</h2>
    <p>
      Everything you watched on the last sheet ran inside one browser tab.
      Real replicas need someone to decide the order they all agree on, and
      that is what a <strong>document</strong> is: a server-sequenced container
      addressed by a <code>tenant</code> and a <code>document</code> id. Every
      client that opens the same pair joins the same live state.
    </p>
    <p>
      Connecting replays the document’s op history, so you start from the
      current converged state rather than an empty board, then holds the
      connection open to stream every later change — yours and everyone
      else’s — in one server-assigned order.
    </p>
    <span class="g-file">src/retro_tutorial_lustre.gleam</span>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={devConstants} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={start} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
      <figcaption class="annot">
        <code>document_on_navigate</code> reads <code>?document=</code> from the
        URL, or mints a name and puts it there — so two tabs share a board by
        sharing the link
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Connecting is a declared effect, not a callback</h2>
    <p>
      The browser facade speaks in callbacks: <code>on_ready</code>, subscription
      handlers, presence events. Bridged into Lustre by hand, each one has to
      <code>dispatch</code> — and dispatching from inside <code>update</code>
      clobbers the model you are in the middle of returning.{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/tylerbutler/watershed/tree/main/watershed_lustre">
      <code>watershed_lustre</code></a> does that bridging once, correctly, so
      your <code>init</code> returns a connection the way it returns any other
      effect.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={connectEffect} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <div class="g-note">
      <p>
        <strong><code>connect_dev</code> is for your laptop.</strong> It mints a
        dev token in the browser from a shared secret, which is exactly what you
        want against <code>just integration-up</code> and exactly what you do not
        want in production. There, issue the token from a backend and call
        <code>watershed_lustre.connect</code> instead. The rest of this sheet is
        identical either way.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>The handle and the handshake are two different events</h2>
    <p>
      Notice that <code>connect_dev</code> names <em>two</em> messages.
      <code>got_document</code> fires as soon as the handle exists;
      <code>connected</code> fires when the handshake and history replay are
      done. Treating those as one event is a first-load race: you get a
      <code>Document</code> that is not yet synced, seed channels against it, and
      wonder why the board flickers.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the tutorial app keeps both, and bootstraps on whichever arrives
      second. That is the whole reason for the double <code>case</code> below.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={readiness} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Seed the channels with <code>ensure_or_map</code></h2>
    <p>
      A fresh document is empty. Two people opening a new board at the same
      moment both want to create the <code>notes</code> map; exactly one must
      win and the other must adopt the winner.
      <code>ensure_or_map</code> collapses that create-race-retry dance into a
      declaration: from the field alone it seeds the channel if it is absent and
      resolves the existing one if it is not.
    </p>
    <p>
      The schema file is the only place key strings live. Two channel fields, one
      plain field, and a phantom <code>BoardDoc</code> tag that scopes all three
      to this document — read a field against the wrong document and it is a
      compile error, not a runtime surprise.
    </p>
    <span class="g-file">src/doc_schema.gleam</span>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={schemaSource} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <span class="g-file">src/retro_tutorial_lustre.gleam</span>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={bootstrap} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <p>
      The two modes are the two races from step 01, declared.
      <code>RegisterMode</code> stores a string per key, which is where whole
      notes go in step 03. <code>TallyMode</code> stores a signed integer per key,
      which is where votes go in step 04. The mode is fixed when the channel is
      created, so this is the moment you choose.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Render only when both channels are home</h2>
    <p>
      Each <code>ensure_or_map</code> dispatches its own handle back, and they do
      not arrive together. Rendering from a half-assembled board would mean a
      notes list with no vote totals, so the app holds the handles in a
      <code>PendingShared</code> pair and does nothing until both are in.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={assemble} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <p>
      The subscriptions come last, and they matter more than they look.
      A subscription fires on <em>subsequent</em> change and never replays what
      is already there — which is why <code>assemble</code> takes a snapshot in
      the same breath. Skip that and the board boots empty and stays empty until
      someone else types.
    </p>
    <div class="g-note">
      <p>
        <strong>One message for both origins.</strong> A local edit and a remote
        edit both dispatch <code>SharedChanged</code>, and both re-read the board
        through one render path. There is no “my write” branch, because the
        moment you write one you start maintaining two versions of the truth.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h3>Deepening: the same thing on the BEAM</h3>
    <p>
      watershed is BEAM-first and the same pure core compiles to the browser.
      Structure operations are identical; only the connection and subscription
      shapes differ, and they differ because the runtimes do.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={beam} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
      <figcaption class="annot">
        watershed_beam · for comparison only, the tutorial stays in the browser
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      On the BEAM there is no readiness race to design around: <code>connect</code>
      {" "}blocks, so a handle you hold is a handle that has replayed. That is the
      one real difference, and it is why the browser path needs the two-message
      pattern above.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Run it</h2>
    <p>
      Here is the whole project, so you know where each later sheet is putting
      its file. Nothing below <code>src/</code> exists yet except the schema and
      the app shell.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={layout} lang="text" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <p>
      watershed is not published to Hex yet, so the two <code>path</code>
      dependencies resolve because this example lives in <code>examples/</code>
      inside a watershed checkout. In your own project take both as git
      dependencies pinned to a commit; the{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/tylerbutler/watershed#install">README</a> gives
      the exact lines.
    </p>
    <span class="g-file">gleam.toml</span>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={gleamToml} lang="toml" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <p>
      Start a floodgate development server on port <code>4000</code> with{" "}
      <code>just integration-up</code>, then:
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code
        code={`cd examples/retro_tutorial_lustre
pnpm install
pnpm build
pnpm serve      # http://localhost:8080`}
        lang="sh"
        theme={watershedCodeTheme}
      />
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="g-out">
    <span class="annot">You can now</span>
    <p>
      Open <code>http://localhost:8080</code> and read the status line:
      <code>connected · board ready · 0 notes</code>. The document is live and
      both channels are attached — there is just nothing in them yet. Step 03
      fixes that.
    </p>
  </div>
</GuideLayout>
